\p
\v 23 \add ≈Around\add* the end of¦210753 the year¦210754, the Aramean army¦210757 \add ≈attacked\add* Yehudah¦210762 and Yerushalem¦210763, and \add ≈killed\add* all the people's¦210770 leaders¦210769. They sent¦210775 all their plunder¦210774 \add back to their\add* king¦210776 in Damascus¦210777.
\v 24 \add ≈Actually, the Arameans¦210785 only had¦210793 a smallish¦210780 army, but¦210786 Yahweh allowed them to defeat the much larger\add* army \add from Yehudah\add* and¦210799 \add ≈bring punishment to\add* \add King\add* Yoash¦210801, because¦210792 they'd abandoned¦210793 Yahweh, their ancestors' god¦210797.
\v 25 When¦210805 they \add ≈withdrew\add*, they left¦210809 \add @Yoash\add* with severe wounds¦210811, and then¦210805 his servants¦210816 conspired¦210814 against him because¦210807 he'd killed the \add #son\add* of the priest¦210820 Yehoyada¦210819. \add ≈So¦210805\add* they killed him in his bed¦210824, and \add although\add* they buried him in ‘The¦210827 City¦210827 of David¦210828’, he wasn't buried \add ≈with the other\add* kings¦210832.
\v 26 The¦210836 ones¦210836 who'd conspired¦210836 to kill him¦210837 were: Zavad¦210838 (a son of the Ammonite¦210842 woman Shimeat¦210841) and¦210843 Yehozavad¦210843 (a son of the Moavite¦210847 woman Shimrit¦210846).
\p
\v 27 \add ≈The list of\add* \add @King¦210864 Yoash's\add* sons¦210849, the many prophecies concerning him, and¦210849 \add &his¦210867\add* renovations of \add ≈the temple\add*, are¦210858 all written¦210858 on¦210859 ‘The Scroll¦210862 of the Kings¦210863’. His¦210867 son¦210866 Amatsyah¦210865 replaced him as king¦210864.
\c 25
\s1 Yehudah's King Amatsyah
\rem /s1 King Amaziah of Judah; Reign of Amaziah; Amaziah Rules in Judah; Amaziah King of Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 14:2-6)
\p
\v 1 Amatsyah¦210876 was twenty-five when he became¦210875 \add Yehudah's\add* king¦210875, and he reigned¦210880 from Yerushalem for twenty-nine years¦210874. His¦210883 mother¦210883 was Yehoaddin¦210884 from Yerushalem.
\v 2 He¦210887 \add ≈mostly followed Yahweh's¦210890 instructions, but¦210887 not¦210892 whole-heartedly\add*.
\p
\v 3 \add ≈Once his rule was established\add*, he¦210901 killed¦210901 \add those of\add* his servants¦210904 who'd \add ≈assassinated\add* his father¦210909 \add Yoash\add*.
\v 4 \add ≈However,\add* he didn't¦210914 order their sons to¦210915 be executed¦210915, because he obeyed Yahweh's¦210924 instructions¦210923 that¦210921 had been written¦210917 down by Mosheh¦210920 \add !(Moses¦210920)\add*, “Parents mustn't die \add ≈because of what their children did\add*, and¦210911 children mustn't die \add ≈because of what their parents¦210929 did\add*, but¦210911 each¦210941 \add ≈individual should be punished for¦210942 their own¦210942 disobedience\add*.”\x + \xo 25:4: \xt Deu 24:16.\x*
\rem /s1 Slaughter of the Edomites; War against Edom
\rem /r (2 Kings 14.7)
\p
\v 5 Then Amatsyah¦210947 \add ≈summoned\add* Yehudah's¦210950 \add men\add* and appointed¦210951 some, \add ≈clan by clan\add*, to be leaders of hundreds¦210971 and of thousands¦210972, for all¦210959 of Yehudah and Benyamin¦210962. \add ≈They counted\add* three-hundred¦210969 thousand¦210972 men who were twenty¦210965 and over, and \add ≈suitable for fighting\add* with a spear¦210977 and shield¦210978.
\v 6 He¦210980 \add ≈also\add* hired¦210980 a hundred thousand¦210983 \add ≈powerful warriors\add* from Yisrael¦210981 for¦210986 three tonnes of¦210987 silver¦210989.
\p
\v 7 \add ≈However,\add* a man¦210991 of¦210991 God came¦210993 and¦210991 told him¦210994, “\add ≈Your majesty\add*, don't¦210997 let¦210999 Yisrael's¦211002 \add ≈soldiers\add* go¦210999 with you¦211000, because¦211003 Yahweh¦211005 isn't¦210997 with Yisrael—those Efrayimites.\f + \fr 25:7 \ft The northern kingdom of Yisrael also came to be known as ‘Efrayim’ (similar how the area of the tribes of Yehudah and Benyamin (plus others who moved in—see 11:16) became known as ‘Yehudah’).\f*
\v 8 \add ≈Even if¦211014\add* you go¦211016 \add and\add* \add ≈fight courageously in¦211029\add* battle¦211020, God will¦211021 cause you \add ≈to be defeated\add* by the enemy¦211024, because¦211025 God has the power to help¦211030 and \add ≡the power to\add* overthrow¦211031.”
\p
\v 9 “\add ≈So¦211033\add* what¦211037 should I do¦211039 about¦211040 \add ≈all the silver\add* that I've \add already\add* paid¦211043 to¦211044 the troops¦211044 from Yisrael¦211045?” Amatsyah¦211034 \add ≈asked\add*.
\p “Yahweh¦211050 \add ≈can do much¦211053 more¦211054 for\add* you than¦211054 that,” God's man \add ≈replied¦211033\add*.
\p
\v 10 \add ≈So¦211056\add* \add King\add* Amatsyah¦211057 \add ≈told\add* the¦211058 troops¦211058 from¦211063 \add ≈Yisrael\add* to go¦211064 home \add again\add*, \add ≈but¦211056\add* they were \add ≈extremely angry\add* with¦211069 Yehudah¦211069, and¦211056 they got home \add still\add* \add ≈feeling furious\add*.
\p
\v 11 Then Amatsyah¦211077 \add ≈took courage\add* and led¦211079 his \add ≈army\add* out to the Salt¦211085 Valley¦211084, \add ≈where\add* \add #they\add* \add ≈slaughtered\add* ten¦211092 thousand¦211093 \add ≈Edomite\add* \add soldiers\add*.
\v 12 Yehudah¦211100 \add ≈rounded up another ten¦211095 thousand¦211096\add* and¦211095 \add ≈herded\add* them to the¦211102 top¦211102 of¦211102 a cliff¦211103, \add ≈where they¦211104 drove them over to kill them\add*.
\p
\v 13 \add ≈However,\add* \add during that time\add*, the \add ≈warriors\add* that Amatsyah¦211116 had¦211115 sent back \add to Yisrael\add* raided¦211120 Yehudah's¦211122 cities¦211121 \add all the way\add* from¦211123 Shomron¦211123 \add !(Samaria¦211123)\add* to Beyt¦211126-Horon¦211127. They'd¦211127 \add ≈killed\add* three¦211130 thousand¦211131 \add people\add* and taken \add back\add* a large amount of¦211130 plunder¦211133.
\s1 Amatsyah worships false gods
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 14 After¦211138 \add King\add* Amatsyah¦211140 returned from¦211141 \add ≈fighting\add* the¦211144 Edomites, he¦211145 brought¦211145 \add back\add* their¦211154 \add idols\add* and set¦211151 them up¦211151 to be \add his\add* gods—bowing¦211155 down to them and offering sacrifices.
\v 15 \add ≈That made\add* Yahweh¦211162 \add ≈very\add* angry at¦211163 Amatsyah¦211163, and¦211159 he sent¦211164 a prophet¦211166 to tell him, “Why \add ≈did you¦211170 want those\add* gods¦211173 who weren't \add ≈even able to save their¦211181 own¦211181 people¦211181 from¦211182 your army\add*?”
\p
\v 16 While he¦211200 was still speaking¦211186, \add @the¦211199 king¦211191\add* \add ≈snapped at¦211186\add* him, “\add ≈Who asked you to¦211206 be my¦211213\add* counsellor¦211190? \add ≈Stop right this minute or\add* they'll strike¦211197 you down¦211197!”
\p \add ≈So¦211184\add* the prophet¦211199 stopped¦211198 \add talking\add*, \add ≈but¦211184 then¦211184 he added\add*, “I¦211201 know¦211201 that God¦211205 has¦211204 \add ≈decided\add* to destroy¦211206 you because you've¦211209 done¦211209 that, and¦211184 \add ≡because\add* you didn't¦211211 listen¦211212 to my advice¦211213.”
\s1 Yehudah brings trouble on itself
\rem /s1 War against Israel; Israel Defeats Judah
\s4 Both kingdoms
\rem /r (2 Kings 14.8-20)
\p
\v 17 \add ≈Some time later,\add* Yehudah's¦211219 King Amatsyah¦211217 \add ≈consulted his advisors\add*, then sent¦211220 \add a challenge\add* to¦211221 Yisrael's¦211231 King Yoash¦211223 (son of Yehoahaz¦211226, son of Yehu¦211229), “Let's face each other \add on the battlefield\add*.”
\v 18 \add ≈But¦211238\add* Yisrael's¦211242 King¦211240 Yoash¦211239 \add ≈replied\add*, “A thistle in Lebanon sent¦211253 \add a message\add* to a cedar¦211256 in Lebanon, saying, ‘Give¦211260 your¦211264 daughter¦211264 to my¦211265 son¦211265 for¦211266 a wife,’ but¦211238 some \add random\add* animal¦211268 passed¦211267 through the Lebanese countryside¦211269 and¦211238 trampled¦211272 the thistle.
\v 19 You¦211279 \add ≈defeated¦211279\add* Edom¦211282 and \add ≈now¦211286 it's¦211283 gone to your head\add*. \add ≈So¦211278 stay¦211287 in¦211288 your chair at¦211288 home¦211288 and don't get over-excited, otherwise both you and Yehudah¦211294 will be toppled\add*.”
\p
\v 20 \add ≈But¦211297\add* \add King\add* Amatsyah¦211300 \add ≈took no notice\add*, because¦211301 God¦211302 \add ≈was¦211302 planning for him to¦211305 be defeated for worshipping\add* Edom's¦211311 gods¦211310.
\v 21 \add ≈So¦211313\add* Yisrael's¦211317 King¦211315 Yoash¦211314 \add ≈advanced\add*, and¦211313 \add ≈the¦211315 two armies faced each other\add* at¦211318 Beyt-Shemesh in¦211325 Yehudah¦211324,
\v 22 and Yehudah¦211331 was¦211330 \add ≈badly defeated¦211330\add* by Yisrael¦211333, and \add ≈all their warriors fled¦211334 back home\add*.
\v 23 Yisrael's¦211353 King Yoash captured King Amatsyah¦211339 (son of Yoash, son of Yehoahaz¦211348) at¦211359 Beyt-Shemesh and¦211338 took him¦211356 to Yerushalem. Then¦211338 he tore down Yerushalem's¦211357 wall¦211359 from¦211361 the Efrayim¦211362 Gate to the Corner Gate—a section about¦211340 180m long.
\v 24 He¦211382 took back to Shomron¦211396 \add !(Samaria¦211396)\add* all¦211371 the gold¦211373 and silver¦211374 and \add ≈other valuable items that¦211379 Oved¦211385-Edom¦211386 had been guarding in¦211380 the temple\add*, as well as the valuables from the \add ≈palace\add* and some hostages¦211394.
\rem /s1 Death of Amaziah
\p
\v 25 After the death¦211407 of Yisrael's¦211413 King Yoash¦211403 (son¦211409 of Yehoahaz¦211411), Yehudah's¦211405 King Amatsyah¦211400 lived¦211399 for a further fifteen years¦211416.
\v 26 \add The account\add* of everything else Amatsyah¦211420 did \add ≈was written¦211425\add* in the scroll¦211428 of ‘The Kings¦211429 of Yehudah¦211431 and¦211418 Yisrael¦211432’.
\v 27 From¦211434 the¦211434 time¦211434 that Amatsyah¦211438 had turned¦211437 from¦211439 following¦211439 Yahweh¦211440, \add ≈there was a conspiracy¦211443 to assassinate him\add* in¦211444 Yerushalem¦211444, \add ≈but¦211434\add* he¦211437 fled¦211445 to Lakish. \add ≈However,\add* they \add ≈traced him\add* to Lakish and¦211434 killed¦211450 him there.
\v 28 They used horses¦211456 to carry¦211453 his body back \add to Yerushalem\add*, and¦211457 he was buried¦211457 with¦211462 his ancestors¦211461 in¦211462 ‘The¦211462 City¦211462 of¦211462 David’.\f + \fr 25:28 \ft The Hebrew actually has the unusual ‘The City of Yehudah’ here.\f*
\c 26
\s1 Yehudah's King Uzziyah
\rem /s1 King Uzziah of Judah; Uzziah Rules in Judah; Reign of Uzziah; Uzziah King of Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 14:21-22; 15:1-7)
\p
\v 1 Then¦211465 all¦211466 the¦211468 people¦211468 took¦211465 sixteen-year-old Uzziyah¦211472 and¦211465 made¦211479 him¦211480 king¦211479 to replace his¦211482 father¦211482 Amatsyah¦211483.
\v 2 \add @Uzziyah\add* restored¦211490 Eylat City to¦211491 Yehudah¦211491 and¦211490 rebuilt it¦211490 after¦211492 \add ≈his¦211498 father's¦211498 death\add*.
\p
\v 3 Uzziyah¦211506 was sixteen when¦211507 he became¦211507 king¦211507, and he reigned¦211511 from Yerushalem for fifty-two¦211508 years¦211505. His¦211514 mother¦211514 was Yekolyah from Yerushalem.
\v 4 \add He\add* did \add ≈the¦211522 things that¦211526 Yahweh¦211524 said were good, like\add* his¦211530 father¦211530 Amatsyah¦211529 had¦211528 done¦211528.
\v 5 He¦211532 \add ≈strived to¦211533 follow\add* God \add ≈during the lifetime of¦211535\add* Zekaryah¦211536, who¦211537 instructed¦211537 him. During the time¦211540 that he¦211545 \add ≈obeyed\add* Yahweh¦211544, God \add ≈made¦211545 him successful¦211545\add*.
\p
\v 6 \add @Uzziyah\add* went¦211549 to attack the Philistines¦211551, and \add successfully\add* broke¦211552 through the walls at¦211551 Gat, Yavneh, and Ashdod. He¦211563 \add ≈rebuilt\add* Ashdod \add ≈and other\add* cities¦211564 in¦211551 the Philistia \add region\add*.
\v 7 God helped¦211568 him¦211568 against the Philistines¦211572, against the Arabians¦211575 who¦211577 were¦211577 living¦211577 in¦211578 Gurbaal, and¦211568 \add ≡against\add* the Meunites¦211581.
\v 8 The¦211584 Ammonites¦211584 paid¦211583 tribute¦211585 to¦211586 Uzziyah¦211586, and¦211583 his¦211588 \add ≈fame spread\add* as far as the¦211591 Egyptian¦211593 border because¦211594 he¦211595 was becoming more powerful.
\p
\v 9 \add King\add* Uzziyah¦211601 built¦211600 \add ≈fortified¦211615\add* towers¦211602 in Yerushalem¦211603 at the Corner¦211607 Gate, the Valley¦211611 Gate, and¦211600 at the angle¦211614 in the wall.
\v 10 He \add ≈also\add* built¦211617 towers¦211618 in the wilderness¦211619, and dug¦211620 a lot of wells¦211621, because¦211623 he had a lot of cattle—both in the lowlands and in the plains¦211630. He was \add ≈also interested in horticulture, so\add* he had workers stationed in his vineyards and in his fertile¦211634 fields.
\p
\v 11 \add ≈Uzziyah's¦211643 army was trained for fighting\add* battles, and organised into divisions¦211649 set up by Yeiel¦211653 the scribe¦211655 and the commander¦211662 Maaseyah¦211656, under the supervision of Hananyah¦211661, one¦211662 of the king's¦211663 officials¦211657.
\v 12 There were 2,600 \add ≈clan leaders\add* who led the \add ≈powerful warriors¦211669\add*,
\v 13 and¦211675 \add the full force of\add* 37,500 \add ≈was under¦211675 their¦211677 authority\add*, to support the king¦211692 against his \add #enemies\add*.
\v 14 Uzziyah¦211699 \add ≈supplied\add* \add #shields¦211703, spears¦211704, helmets¦211705, armoured¦211706 vests, bows¦211707, and¦211697 slingshots¦211709\add* for¦211698 the¦211702 entire army¦211702.
\v 15 \add ≈Using a local\add* invention¦211715, he made war¦211714 machines in¦211713 Yerushalem¦211713 to be¦211717 placed on¦211713 the towers¦211720 and corners¦211723 \add of the walls\add* to fire arrows¦211725 and large¦211727 stones¦211726. His¦211729 fame \add now\add* \add ≈spread widely\add* because¦211733 he'd received a lot of help¦211736 that had made him very powerful.
\rem /s1 Uzziah Is Punished for His Pride; Uzziah's Sin and Punishment; Pride and Apostasy
\p
\v 16 \add ≈However¦211749,\add* \add at the peak of\add* his strength¦211742, he \add ≈became very arrogant, and that led to his\add* destruction¦211747. He¦211748 \add ≈disobeyed\add* his god¦211750 Yahweh¦211755 and went¦211751 into¦211752 the \add ≈temple¦211754\add* to burn¦211756 incense on¦211749 the incense altar¦211759.
\v 17 The¦211769 high priest¦211765 Azaryah¦211764 and¦211762 eighty¦211770 other \add ≈brave\add* priests¦211767 went¦211762 in after¦211763 him.
\v 18 They \add ≈confronted\add* King¦211779 Uzziyah and¦211775 \add ≈challenged\add* him¦211781, “It's¦211781 not¦211782 \add permitted\add* for you, Uzziyah, to sacrifice to Yahweh, only for Aharon's¦211792 descendants¦211790 the priests¦211789—the ones consecrated¦211793 to sacrifice. Leave the sanctuary¦211798, because¦211788 you've¦211800 \add ≈disobeyed\add* Yahweh God¦211806, \add ≈so now\add* he won't¦211782 honour¦211804 you.”\x + \xo 26:18: \xt Exo 30:7-8; Num 3:10.\x*
\p
\v 19 Then¦211808 Uzziyah¦211809, who was holding an incense pan, became¦211813 very angry, \add ≈but¦211808\add* when¦211813 he \add started\add* raging at¦211810 the priests, spots of leprosy¦211817 \add suddenly\add* appeared on¦211810 his¦211819 forehead¦211819 while he was still there beside the incense altar¦211825 in¦211810 front¦211820 of the priests.
\v 20 The high priest¦211831 Azaryah¦211830 looked¦211828 \add more closely\add*, and¦211828 \add ≈confirming that it was indeed\add* leprosy on¦211840 his¦211840 forehead¦211840, hurried¦211846 him outside. \add @The king\add* was \add now also\add* in¦211840 a hurry, because¦211848 Yahweh¦211850 had¦211849 afflicted¦211849 him.
\p
\v 21 King¦211854 Uzziyah¦211853 had leprosy until¦211857 he died, \add ≈so\add* he had to live in an isolated residence and wasn't allowed to \add ≈approach the temple\add*. His son¦211871 Yotam¦211870 \add !(Jotham¦211870)\add* \add ≈stood in for him\add*—supervising the \add ≈palace\add* and \add ≈ruling Yehudah\add*.
\p
\v 22 The record of all the other things Uzziyah¦211884 did \add ≈while he¦211888 was king\add* was written¦211888 by the prophet¦211893 Yeshayah¦211889 \add !(Isaiah¦211889)\add* (son¦211890 of Amots¦211892).
\v 23 \add ≈When¦211895 Uzziyah¦211896 died\add*, because¦211909 of¦211899 his leprosy they¦211910 buried¦211900 him with¦211905 his ancestors¦211899 in a \add ≈royal¦211908\add* grave¦211906 in the countryside¦211905, and¦211895 his son¦211915 Yotam¦211914 replaced him as king¦211913.\x + \xo 26:23: \xt Isa 6:1.\x*
\c 27
\s1 Yehudah's King Yotam
\rem /s1 Jotham Rules in Judah; Reign of Jotham; King Jotham of Judah; Jotham King of Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 15:32-38)
\p
\v 1 Yotam¦211924 \add !(Jotham¦211924)\add* was twenty-five¦211921 when¦211925 he became¦211925 king¦211925, and¦211926 he reigned¦211930 from Yerushalem¦211931 for sixteen years¦211923. His¦211933 mother¦211933 was Yerusha¦211934, daughter¦211935 of \add the priest\add* Tsadok¦211937.
\v 2 He¦211939 did \add ≈the things that Yahweh said were good\add* like his¦211948 father¦211948 Uzziyah¦211947 had¦211946 done¦211946 (except that he¦211952 didn't¦211950 \add ≈trespass\add* in Yahweh's¦211942 temple¦211955). \add ≈However,\add* the people¦211958 were still¦211957 behaving¦211959 corruptly¦211959.
\p
\v 3 He¦211962 rebuilt the \add ≈temple's\add* Upper Gate and¦211970 \add ≈extended\add* the Ofel¦211971 wall¦211970.
\v 4 He¦211976 built cities¦211975 in the Yehudah¦211979 hill¦211977 country¦211977, and built fortresses¦211982 and towers¦211983 in the forests.
\v 5 He \add ≈attacked\add* the Ammonites' king¦211989 and \add ≈dominated\add* them¦211994. \add ≈Then¦211985 for the next three years¦212020\add*, the Ammonites brought¦212015 him three tonnes of silver¦212006, and 2,000 tonnes each of wheat¦212010 and barley¦212011 each year¦212001.
\v 6 Yotam¦212025 became more powerful because¦212026 he'd established \add ≈a pattern of obeying\add* his god¦212031 Yahweh¦212030.
\v 7 The record of all the other things Yotam¦212035 did \add ≈while he was king\add*, including¦212036 all his battles¦212038, was written¦212041 on¦212042 the scroll¦212044 ‘The kings¦212045 of Yisrael¦212047 and¦212033 Yehudah¦212048’.
\v 8 He¦212055 was¦212055 twenty-five¦212052 when¦212056 he became¦212056 king¦212056, and¦212057 he reigned¦212061 from Yerushalem¦212062 for sixteen years¦212054.
\v 9 Then¦212064 \add (at forty-one years old)\add*, Yotam¦212065 \add ≈died\add* and was buried¦212069 in¦212071 ‘The¦212071 City¦212071 of¦212071 David¦212072’, and his¦212076 son¦212075 Ahaz¦212074 replaced him¦212076 as king¦212073.
\c 28
\s1 Yehudah's King Ahaz
\rem /s1 Reign of Ahaz; King Ahaz of Judah; Ahaz Rules in Judah; Ahaz King of Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 16:1-4)
\p
\v 1 Ahaz¦212083 was¦212079 twenty-five¦212081 when¦212084 he became¦212084 king¦212084, and¦212085 he reigned¦212089 from Yerushalem¦212090 for sixteen years¦212082, \add ≈but¦212085\add* he didn't¦212091 do¦212093 what Yahweh¦212096 \add ≈wanted\add*, like¦212097 his¦212098 ancestor David¦212097 had.
\v 2 \add ≈Instead,\add* he¦212100 followed the example¦212101 of¦212102 Yisrael's¦212103 kings¦212102, \add ≈including¦212104\add* casting metal idols for the Baal¦212107.
\v 3 He offered incense in¦212111 the Ben-Hinnom¦212114 valley¦212111 and¦212109 he burnt¦212115 his¦212118 children with¦212111 fire¦212119,\f + \fr 28:3 \ft Probably, but not definitely, referring to the practice of child sacrifice.\f* following the detestable¦212120 customs of the nations¦212121 that¦212122 Yahweh¦212124 had driven away \add ≈as the Israelis¦212126 had entered the region\add*,
\v 4 plus¦212130 he¦212129 \add offered\add* sacrifices¦212129 and burnt incense¦212130 at¦212131 the hilltop shrines¦212131, and on¦212131 the hills¦212134, and under¦212135 every¦212136 large, green tree¦212138.
\s1 War between Syria and Yisrael
\rem /s1 War with Syria and Israel; Aram and Israel Defeat Judah
\s4 Both kingdoms
\r (2 Kgs 16:5)
\p
\v 5 \add ≈So¦212141\add* his¦212143 god¦212143 Yahweh \add ≈allowed\add* \add @King¦212145 Ahaz\add* \add ≈to be defeated¦212165 by\add* Aram's¦212146 king—they attacked and took¦212150 many prisoners \add back\add* to Damascus¦212155. \add ≈In¦212157 addition¦212141,\add* \add @Yahweh\add* \add ≈allowed Yisrael's¦212160 king to be victorious, and they slaughtered many fighters\add*.\x + \xo 28:5-6: \xt 2Ki 16:5; Isa 7:1.\x*
\v 6 Yisrael's King Pekah¦212170 (Remalyah's¦212173 son¦212171) killed¦212169 120,000 \add ≈powerful warriors\add* in Yehudah¦212174 in one¦212179 day¦212178, after they'd abandoned¦212184 their¦212189 ancestors'¦212189 god¦212188.
\v 7 A warrior¦212194 from Efrayim¦212195 \add named\add* Zikri¦212192 killed¦212191 King¦212201 Ahaz's son¦212199 Maaseyah¦212198, Azrikam¦212204 the \add ≈palace¦212206 supervisor\add*, and¦212191 Elkanah¦212209 the king's second-in-command¦212210.
\rem /s1 Intervention of Oded
\v 8 Yisrael's¦212217 soldiers captured two-hundred thousand¦212220 of their relatives¦212218, including¦212214 their wives and \add ≈children\add*. They also took¦212214 a lot of plunder¦212228 back to Shomron¦212234 \add !(Samaria¦212234)\add* with them.
\s2 The prophet Oded
\rem /s1 The Prophet Oded
\p
\v 9 \add ≈Now\add* there¦212237 was a prophet¦212239 of Yahweh \add ≈named\add* Oded¦212241, and he went¦212243 out¦212243 to \add Yisrael's\add* army¦212245 as it was returning to Shomron¦212247, and told them, “Listen, your ancestors'¦212255 god¦212253, Yahweh, was angry¦212251 with¦212251 Yehudah¦212258, so he \add ≈helped you defeat them\add*. \add ≈However,\add* you \add people\add* killed¦212261 them in¦212251 rage¦212264, \add and\add* \add ≈God¦212253 has¦212267 taken notice\add*.
\v 10 Now¦212286 you want to \add ≈keep the¦212270 people¦212270 of¦212270 Yerushalem¦212273 and Yehudah¦212272 as your own male¦212277 and female¦212278 slaves¦212277\add*, \add ≈but¦212269 that would certainly¦212281 make you people guilty¦212285 in\add* your god¦212287 Yahweh¦212286's view.
\v 11 \add ≈So¦212289 then¦212289,\add* listen¦212290 to¦212290 me¦212290 and¦212291 return¦212291 those captives¦212292, \add because\add* \add ≈they're your own relatives¦212295\add*, and Yahweh¦212300 \add ≈is¦212301 extremely angry at\add* you \add people\add*.”
\p
\v 12 Then \add ≈some of the leaders¦212306 of the Efrayim¦212309 tribe took action\add* and \add confronted\add* \add ≈those returning from battle¦212331\add*: Azaryah¦212310 (Yehohanan's¦212313 son), Berekyah¦212314 (Meshillemot's¦212317 son), Yehizkiyah (Shallum's¦212321 son), and Amasa¦212322 (Hadlai's¦212325 son)
\v 13 told¦212347 \add @the ones returning\add*, “Don't¦212335 bring¦212337 those captives¦212340 here, because¦212342 \add ≈that would be disobedience, and add to the rest of our¦212351 disobedience and wrongdoing\add*, because \add Yahweh's already\add* angry at \add us here in\add* Yisrael¦212364.”
\v 14 \add ≈So\add* the \add returning\add* \add ≈soldiers¦212368\add* released the captives¦212371 in¦212375 front¦212375 of¦212375 the leaders and all the assembled¦212379 \add people\add*, and \add ≈dropped all the plunder¦212374\add* \add there as well\add*.
\v 15 Then some men \add from Yisrael\add* \add ≈were called out by¦212386 name to come and search the plunder¦212395 to find clothes and\add* dress the naked¦212391 captives¦212388 \add from Yehudah\add* and give them sandals¦212397. Then they \add ≈gave them food and drink¦212399, as well as oil\add* \add to rub on their wounds\add*. They gave donkeys¦212402 to those who \add ≈couldn't easily walk\add*, and took¦212387 them to Yeriho¦212407 (\add Jericho,\add* the City¦212408 of Palms) which was nearer their relatives¦212412, then¦212381 those men returned¦212413 to Shomron¦212414 \add !(Samaria¦212414)\add*.
\rem /s1 Ahaz Closes the Temple; Assyria Refuses to Help Judah; Ahaz Asks Assyria for Help
\rem /r (2 Kings 16.7-9)
\p
\v 16 Around that¦212418 time¦212417, King¦212420 Ahaz¦212421 \add ≈requested help¦212426 from\add* the Assyrian kings¦212424,
\v 17 because the¦212430 Edomites¦212430 had¦212431 been and attacked¦212432 Yehudah¦212433 and taken¦212434 captives¦212436.
\v 18 \add ≈Also¦212438\add* the Philistines had¦212439 raided the lowland cities¦212440 and the Negev¦212442, and they'd captured¦212444 Beyt¦212447-Shemesh¦212449, Ayyalon¦212452, and Gederoth¦212455, as well as Sokoh¦212458, Timnah¦212462, and Gimzo¦212466 with¦212440 their surrounding villages¦212469,
\v 19 because¦212485 Yahweh was humbling¦212475 Yehudah¦212479 because King¦212482 Ahaz¦212481 had \add ≈thrown off\add* restraint¦212486 \add ≈there\add* and¦212488 been very unfaithful¦212488 to Yahweh.
\v 20 \add ≈So¦212492\add* the¦212496 Assyrian King¦212496 Tiglat¦212494-Pileser came¦212492, \add ≈but¦212492\add* he¦212498 \add ended up\add* \add ≈adding to¦212499 his troubles rather than helping\add*.
\v 21 Ahaz¦212506 gave¦212516 the Assyrian king¦212514 \add ≈valuables from the temple and the palace and from other leaders\add*, \add ≈but¦212511 that didn't¦212519\add* help¦212520.
\s1 Ahaz's disobedience
\rem /s1 Apostasy and Death of Ahaz; The Sins of Ahaz
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 22 \add ≈While King¦212530 Ahaz¦212531 was experiencing those troubles, he¦212526 disobeyed Yahweh¦212528 even more\add*
\v 23 and sacrificed¦212533 to the gods of Damascus¦212535 (\add ≈because¦212539 Aram¦212543 had¦212536 defeated¦212536 him¦212537\add*). He¦212538 \add ≈reasoned\add*, “Since the gods of the Aramean kings¦212541 helped them, I'll¦212548 sacrifice¦212548 to them, and then¦212533 they'll¦212549 help¦212549 me¦212549.” \add ≈However,\add* that¦212536 led to Ahaz's fall, and \add ≡to the fall of\add* all¦212555 of Yehudah.\f + \fr 28:23 \ft Sometimes in 2 Chronicles, Yehudah is referred to as Yisrael, but as that can be confusing for readers, we've made adjustments.\f*
\v 24 Then Ahaz¦212560 gathered¦212559 all the furnishings \add that were used\add* in¦212587 the \add ≈temple\add*, and broke them into pieces¦212567 and locked the \add ≈temple\add* doors¦212577. Then he set up \add ≈pagan\add* altars¦212583 at¦212587 every Yerushalem¦212587 \add ≈intersection\add*,
\v 25 and¦212589 in every Yehudah¦212593 city¦212591 he¦212594 set up hilltop¦212595 shrines¦212595 to make¦212596 sacrifices to other¦212598 gods¦212597, \add ≈thus\add* angering¦212599 Yahweh¦212602, his ancestors'¦212604 god¦212603.
\p
\v 26 The record of all¦212608 the other things Ahaz did \add ≈while he was king\add* was written¦212614 on¦212615 the scroll¦212617 ‘The kings¦212618 of Yehudah¦212620 and¦212606 Yisrael¦212621’.
\v 27 Then Ahaz¦212624 died and¦212623 was buried¦212628 in ‘The City¦212629 of David’, but¦212623 not in the tombs¦212634 of the \add other\add* kings¦212635 of Yisrael¦212636. Then his¦212640 son¦212639 Hizkiyah¦212638 \add ≈replaced him¦212640 as king¦212637\add*.\x + \xo 28:27: \xt Isa 14:28.\x*
\c 29
\s1 Purifying the temple
\rem /s1 Hezekiah and his successors; Hezekiah Rules in Judah; Hezekiah Purifies the Temple; Reign of Hezekiah; King Hezekiah of Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 18:1-3)
\p
\v 1 Hizkiyah¦212643 was twenty-five when he became¦212644 king¦212644, and he reigned¦212653 from Yerushalem¦212654 for twenty-nine years¦212649. His¦212656 mother¦212656 was Zekaryah's¦212660 daughter¦212658 Aviyah¦212657.
\v 2 He did what \add ≈pleased\add* Yahweh¦212665, like his¦212671 ancestor \add King\add* David¦212670 had¦212669 done¦212669.
\rem /s1 The Purification of the Temple; Hezekiah Reopens the Temple; The Temple Cleansed
\p
\v 3 In¦212685 the \add very\add* first¦212675 month¦212677 of¦212676 his¦212676 reign¦212676, he¦212679 \add ≈unlocked\add* the \add ≈temple\add* doors¦212682 and¦212686 repaired¦212686 them¦212686.
\v 4 Then¦212688 he¦212688 \add ≈summoned\add* the priests¦212691 and¦212688 the Levites¦212694, assembled them¦212695 in \add ≈the eastern¦212697 temple courtyard\add*,
\v 5 and told them¦212700, “\add Now you\add* Levites¦212702, listen¦212701 to me¦212701. \add You need to\add* consecrate yourselves¦212704 now¦212703, and consecrate the residence of Yahweh¦212709, your ancestors'¦212711 god¦212710, and remove¦212712 \add ≈any\add* defilement¦212715 from¦212716 the sacred¦212718 place¦212718,
\v 6 because¦212720 our ancestors¦212723 were unfaithful¦212722 and \add ≈disobeyed\add* our god¦212729 Yahweh¦212727. Then¦212734 they abandoned¦212730 him¦212730, \add ≈lost interest in this temple\add*, and turned¦212731 \add ≈their¦212732 back\add* \add on it all\add*.
\v 7 They¦212740 extinguished¦212743 the lamps¦212746 and¦212743 \add ≈locked the temple up\add*. \add After that\add*, they didn't¦212748 burn any incense¦212747 or offer¦212750 any burnt¦212750 sacrifices in¦212754 the sacred¦212754 place¦212754 of¦212755 Yisrael's¦212756 god¦212755.
\v 8 \add That's why\add* Yahweh¦212760 \add ≈was¦212758 angry at¦212773\add* Yerushalem¦212764 and \add all of\add* Yehudah¦212763, and \add ≈allowed us to become a place of terror and horror¦212768 and scorn\add*, as¦212770 you've all seen¦212772 with¦212773 your¦212773 \add own\add* eyes¦212773.
\v 9 \add ≈As a result,\add* our fathers¦212777 died \add ≈in¦212778 battle\add*, and our wives¦212781 and \add ≈children\add* have¦212776 been \add ≈captured and taken to other countries\add*.
\p
\v 10 Now \add ≈I sincerely want to make¦212791 an agreement¦212792\add* with Yisrael's¦212795 god¦212794 Yahweh¦212793, \add ≈so that his fierce anger¦212799\add* will¦212796 turn¦212796 away from¦212797 us.
