\p
\v 11 \add ≈So¦214058 then\add* Hizkiyah¦214054 \add ≈ordered\add* \add >them\add* to prepare¦214055 the \add ≈temple\add* storerooms, which they¦214059 did,
\v 12 and they¦214061 brought¦214061 in¦214067 the contributions¦214064 and the tenths \add that had been given\add*, and the \add ≈things¦214066 faithfully¦214067 dedicated¦214066\add* \add to Yahweh\add*. A Levite¦214073, Konanyah¦214070, \add ≈was appointed as supervisor\add*, with¦214067 his brother¦214075 Shimei¦214074 \add ≈as deputy\add*.
\v 13 \add ≈Those two supervised\add* Yehiel, Azazyah¦214079, Nahat¦214080, Asahel¦214081, Yerimot¦214082, Yozavad¦214083, Eliel¦214084, Ismakyah, Mahat¦214086, and Benayah¦214087, by¦214094 the arrangement of¦214094 King¦214096 Hizkiyah¦214095 and Azaryah¦214097 the \add ≈temple supervisor\add*.
\p
\v 14 The Levite¦214107 Imnah's¦214106 son¦214104, Kore, \add ≈who guarded the temple's\add* eastern¦214109 gate¦214108, was¦214110 \add ≈put in charge\add* of¦214111 the free-will¦214111 offering¦214111 to God—to \add ≈distribute¦214113 those offerings¦214111 that were made\add* to Yahweh¦214115, as well as any consecrated items.
\v 15 Eden¦214122, Minyamin¦214123, Yeshua¦214124, Shemayah¦214125, Amaryah¦214126, and¦214119 Shekanyah¦214127 \add ≈faithfully¦214130 assisted\add* him in¦214128 the priests' cities¦214128 to \add ≈distribute¦214131 those things\add* to the \add different\add* divisions¦214133 of priests, \add ≈regardless of their¦214132 status or size\add*.
\v 16 Every¦214149 male who¦214146 was at¦214155 least three¦214141 years¦214142 old, \add ≈and¦214143 was listed in the genealogical¦214138 records, received a portion\add* for their service¦214154 in the \add ≈temple, as each division¦214156 performed their assigned duties¦214155\add*.
\v 17 \add ≈Each priestly¦214160 clan kept genealogical¦214159 records\add* \add and received a portion\add*, and¦214163 so did the¦214163 Levites¦214163 \add ≈who were at¦214168 least twenty¦214165 years¦214166 old\add*, all according¦214161 to¦214167 their¦214168 divisions¦214169,
\v 18 as well as their wives¦214175 and young children¦214174, and sons¦214176 and daughters¦214177 whose names were on¦214182 the genealogies¦214171, because¦214181 they'd¦214183 also faithfully¦214182 consecrated¦214183 themselves¦214183 for the \add ≈sacred¦214185 work\add*.
\v 19 Also¦214187, there were people chosen by¦214200 name to give a portion¦214202 to each male¦214205 among¦214206 Aharon's¦214188 descendants¦214187, the priests¦214206, who¦214198 had¦214199 pasture¦214191 land¦214191 around their¦214192 cities¦214192, as well as to each Levite included on¦214190 the genealogical¦214209 records.
\p
\v 20 So¦214212 \add King\add* Hizkiyah¦214214 did that¦214213 throughout Yehudah¦214217, and¦214212 he continued to¦214222 faithfully¦214221 \add ≈follow\add* his¦214224 god¦214224 Yahweh's¦214223 \add ≈instructions\add*.
\v 21 He¦214236 \add ≈tried hard to be diligent and sincere in whatever he did¦214244\add* for the \add ≈temple operation\add*, as he followed God's¦214240 rules and commands¦214238, and he prospered¦214245.
\c 32
\s1 The Assyrians threaten Yerushalem
\rem /s1 Sennacherib Threatens Jerusalem; Sennacherib's Invasion; Assyria Invades Judah; The Assyrians Threaten Jerusalem
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 18:13-37; 19:14-19,35-37; Isa 36:1-22; 37:8-38)
\p
\v 1 After¦214248 all \add ≈those¦214251 acts of faithfulness¦214250\add* \add by Hizkiyah\add*, the Assyrian King¦214254 Sennacherib¦214253 \add (Heb. Sanheriv)\add* \add ≈invaded¦214257\add* Yehudah¦214258. He \add ≈besieged\add* the fortified¦214263 cities¦214262 and prepared to¦214265 capture them¦214265 for himself.
\v 2 \add ≈When¦214268\add* \add King\add* Hizkiyah¦214269 saw¦214268 that¦214270 Sennacherib¦214273 had¦214272 \add ≈entered\add* \add the country\add* and¦214274 \add clearly\add* \add ≈intended to attack\add* Yerushalem¦214278,
\v 3 he¦214280 consulted¦214280 with¦214281 his¦214283 officials¦214283 and \add ≈army leaders\add* about¦214283 blocking up¦214285 the springs¦214289 outside¦214291 the city¦214292, and they¦214293 helped¦214293 him¦214293.
\v 4 Many people¦214296 gathered¦214295 and blocked the springs¦214304 from feeding the stream¦214307 that¦214308 flowed¦214308 through the area, saying¦214312, “Why should the Assyrian kings¦214315 come¦214314 and find¦214317 \add ≈plenty of water¦214318\add*?”
\v 5 Then¦214321 he¦214321 strengthened \add ≈his fortifications\add*, repairing broken¦214328 \add sections of the\add* wall, and built¦214322 towers¦214332 and an outer wall. He strengthened the \add ≈supporting wall\add* \add on the east side of\add* ‘The City¦214340 of David¦214341’. He \add also\add* manufactured many weapons¦214343 and shields¦214345,
\v 6 and he \add ≈appointed army commanders¦214348\add* and assembled¦214353 them at the plaza by the city¦214359 gate¦214358, and \add ≈encouraged them with these words\add*,
\v 7 “Be¦214366 strong¦214366 and¦214367 be courageous¦214367. Don't¦214368 be scared¦214373 or discouraged¦214373 because of the Assyrian¦214376 king¦214375, or by his massive army, because we have someone on our side who's \add ≈more¦214388 powerful\add* than¦214388 him¦214388.
\v 8 \add ≈They can only rely on human strength, but¦214393 we have\add* our god¦214395 Yahweh¦214394 to¦214396 help¦214396 us and¦214397 fight¦214397 our battles¦214398 \add for us\add*.” \add ≈So\add* the people¦214400 \add ≈took strength\add* from King¦214405 Hizkiyah's¦214404 \add ≈encouragement\add*.
\rem /s1 Sennacherib Threatens Jerusalem
\p
\v 9 \add ≈Later, when\add* the Assyrian King Sennacherib¦214413 was \add ≈surrounding\add* Lakish¦214422 city with¦214438 his army, he sent¦214412 some servants¦214417 to Yerushalem¦214418, to Yehudah's¦214431 King Hizkiyah¦214429 and¦214432 \add ≈all¦214434 the residents\add*, saying¦214439,
\v 10 “\add ≈I,\add*Sennacherib¦214443, the¦214444 king¦214444 of¦214444 Assyria¦214445, \add ≈am asking\add* what¦214448 you're all trusting in as you're staying¦214451 there in Yerushalem¦214453 while it's besieged.
\v 11 Hizkiyah¦214456 tells¦214464 you, ‘Our¦214466 god¦214466 Yahweh¦214465 will¦214467 rescue¦214467 us¦214467 from¦214468 the¦214469 Assyrian¦214470 king¦214469,’ but¦214463 he's misleading¦214457 you, and you'll all die¦214461 of hunger¦214462 and thirst¦214463!
\v 12 Wasn't it Hizkiyah¦214475 who removed¦214476 his hilltop¦214479 shrines¦214479 and his altars¦214482, and spoke¦214483 to \add the people of\add* Yerushalem¦214485 and \add all of\add* Yehudah¦214484, telling you to bow¦214490 down at only one altar¦214488 and offer sacrifices on¦214491 it?
\v 13 Don't¦214498 you people know¦214495 what¦214496 my ancestors¦214499 and I¦214497 have¦214497 done¦214497 to the¦214501 people groups from other countries¦214506? Were¦214504 any¦214500 of¦214501 their¦214511 gods¦214505 able¦214504 to save them from me?
\v 14 Which one of those¦214519 nations'¦214518 gods¦214517 which my ancestors¦214522 destroyed¦214521 is a precedent showing that¦214520 your god¦214532 can rescue¦214525 anyone¦214523 from me?
\v 15 \add ≈So\add* don't¦214543 let Hizkiyah¦214542 deceive¦214540 you or mislead¦214545 you like¦214547 this¦214547. Don't¦214538 believe¦214550 him¦214551, because¦214552 no god of any nation¦214561 or kingdom¦214562 has¦214555 been¦214555 able to save his people¦214564 from my ancestors¦214567 or from me. \add ≈So your god certainly won't¦214538 be able to protect you from my army\add*.
\p
\v 16 Then¦214577 Sennacherib's¦214583 servants¦214579 said \add even\add* more against the¦214583 god Yahweh¦214582, and¦214584 against his¦214586 servant¦214586 Hizkiyah¦214585,
\v 17 plus¦214594 he¦214589 wrote¦214589 letters¦214588 \add ≈insulting\add* Yisrael's¦214593 god Yahweh¦214591 and¦214588 speaking¦214594 against him¦214595, writing, “Just like¦214597 the \add other\add* nations'¦214598 gods¦214597 couldn't save their¦214604 people from me, so¦214606 too Hizkiyah's¦214611 god won't¦214601 be able to \add ≈protect\add* his people from my¦214613 \add ≈strength\add*.”
\v 18 Then¦214615 \add @the Assyrians\add* \add ≈shouted loud\add* \add threats\add* \add ≈in¦214616 Hebrew\add* to the people¦214622 of¦214622 Yerushalem¦214623 who were on¦214616 the wall¦214627, to \add try to\add* frighten¦214628 and¦214629 terrify¦214629 them¦214629 so¦214630 they¦214631 could¦214631 capture¦214631 the city¦214634—
\v 19 \add ≈telling them Yerushalem's¦214640 god was just another god made by people¦214643, and¦214636 just as useless as other countries' gods¦214642\add*.
\rem /s1 Sennacherib's Defeat and Death
\p
\v 20 Then King¦214652 Hizkiyah¦214651 and Yeshayah¦214653 (son¦214654 of¦214654 the prophet¦214657 Amots¦214656) called out¦214661 to the heavens¦214662 \add requesting God's help\add*,
\v 21 and Yahweh¦214666 sent¦214665 a messenger¦214667 who destroyed the powerful warriors, and the leaders and chiefs in the Assyrian \add ≈army\add*, and then their king¦214676 had to¦214681 \add ≈return¦214678 home in\add* shame¦214679. When he¦214682 went¦214682 into the temple of his god¦214684, some¦214685 of \add ≈his sons ran him¦214689 through with¦214675 a\add* sword¦214690.
\p
\v 22 \add ≈So¦214692 that's how\add* Yahweh¦214693 saved¦214692 Hizkiyah¦214696 and Yerushalem's¦214700 inhabitants¦214699 from the Assyrian¦214705 King¦214703 Sennacherib¦214702, and from \add ≈the power of¦214706 other nations\add*, and he¦214709 gave¦214709 them¦214709 peace on all sides.
\v 23 Many¦214712 \add >people\add* came to Yerushalem¦214716 to bring¦214713 offerings¦214714 for Yahweh¦214715 and valuable gifts¦214717 for Yehudah's¦214720 King¦214719 Hizkiyah¦214718, and after¦214726 all¦214723 that, he¦214721 \add ≈was¦214721 highly respected\add* by the¦214725 other nations¦214725.
\s1 Hizkiyah's illness
\rem /s1 Hezekiah's Sickness; Hezekiah's Sickness and Recovery; Hezekiah's Illness and Pride; Hezekiah's Pride, Success and Death
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 20:1-3,12-19; Isa. 38:1-3; 39:1-8)
\p
\v 24 \add ≈Some time¦214731 afterwards,\add* \add King\add* Hizkiyah¦214734 became¦214733 very sick¦214733 \add ≈and was expected to die¦214737, but¦214738\add* he¦214742 prayed¦214738 to Yahweh, and \add @Yahweh\add* \add ≈answered¦214742 him¦214746 with a miracle\add*.
\v 25 \add ≈However,\add* Hizkiyah¦214753 was¦214755 \add ≈very proud\add* and didn't¦214748 \add ≈thank\add* \add >Yahweh\add*, \add ≈so his anger¦214759 fell on¦214758\add* Yerushalem¦214763 and Yehudah¦214762.
\v 26 Then¦214765 Hizkiyah¦214766 and Yerushalem's¦214771 inhabitants¦214770 humbled¦214765 themselves, and so Yahweh's¦214777 anger¦214776 didn't¦214772 \add ≈affect them¦214775 during Hizkiyah's¦214779 lifetime\add*.
\s1 Hizkiyah's wealth
\rem /s1 Hezekiah's Wealth and Splendour; Hezekiah's Prosperity and Achievements
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 27 \add King\add* Hizkiyah¦214782 \add ≈had become very wealthy¦214783, and he¦214788 was highly respected\add*. \add He established\add* treasuries¦214787 for gold¦214792 and silver¦214791, for gemstones and spices¦214795, and for shields¦214796 and other valuable things,
\v 28 as well as storehouses¦214801 for grain¦214803, wine¦214804, and \add olive\add* oil¦214805, and stalls¦214806 for \add &his\add* flocks¦214811 and \add ≈all¦214807 kinds of livestock¦214809\add*.
\v 29 He¦214815 built cities¦214814 for himself, and acquired¦214817 large numbers¦214821 of¦214817 \add ≈sheep and goats, and also cattle\add*, because¦214822 God¦214826 had¦214823 \add ≈made¦214815 him¦214825 very¦214829 prosperous\add*.
\p
\v 30 Hizkiyah¦214832 \add ≈blocked off\add* the upper¦214839 outlet¦214836 at the Gihon¦214838 spring and¦214831 directed¦214840 \add @the water¦214837\add* down the western side of ‘The City¦214844 of David¦214845’. All his¦214850 projects were successful,
\v 31 and so¦214852 when the Babylonian leaders sent¦214857 messengers to \add ≈ask about¦214859 the miracle that¦214861 had¦214862 been done in¦214853 the country\add*, God left¦214864 him \add alone\add* to test¦214866 him and \add ≈see what he really believed\add*.
\s1 Hizkiyah's reign ends
\rem /s1 The End of Hezekiah's Reign; Summary of Hezekiah's Reign
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 20:20-21)
\p
\v 32 The record of all the other things Hizkiyah¦214874 did \add ≈while he was king\add*, \add ≈which includes a record of\add* his¦214875 loyal¦214875 commitment, was written¦214877 on¦214878 the scroll¦214886 ‘The kings¦214887 of Yehudah¦214889 and¦214872 Yisrael¦214890’.
\v 33 Then Hizkiyah¦214893 died and was buried¦214897 in the \add ≈upper tombs\add* with¦214898 David's¦214902 other descendants¦214900, and everyone¦214897 in Yerushalem¦214912 and across Yehudah¦214910 honoured¦214903 him. Then his¦214916 son¦214915 Menashsheh¦214914 replaced him as king¦214913.
\c 33
\s1 Yehudah's king Menashsheh
\rem /s1 Manasseh Rules in Judah; Reign of Manasseh; King Manasseh of Judah; Manasseh King of Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 21:1-9)
\p
\v 1 Menashsheh¦214924 \add !(Manasseh¦214924)\add* was¦214919 twelve when¦214925 he became¦214925 king¦214925, and he reigned¦214929 from Yerushalem¦214930 for fifty-five¦214926 years¦214923.
\v 2 He¦214932 did was what Yahweh \add ≈had said was\add* evil¦214933, like the disgusting things of the \add ≈people¦214942 groups\add* that¦214938 Yahweh had driven out \add ≈ahead of the Israelis¦214942\add*.\x + \xo 33:2: \xt Jer 15:4.\x*
\v 3 The hilltop¦214949 shrines¦214949 that¦214950 his father¦214953 Hizkiyah¦214952 had¦214951 torn down, he turned around and rebuilt them and erected¦214954 altars¦214955 to the Baals¦214956, and he made¦214957 Asherah¦214958 \add poles\add*, and he bowed¦214959 down to all¦214960 the stars in the sky and served¦214964 them.
\v 4 He¦214967 built¦214967 altars¦214968 in Yahweh's¦214970 \add ≈temple\add* in Yerushalem¦214974 that Yahweh had¦214972 previously said¦214972 that his name¦214977 would \add ≈always¦214978 be¦214975 associated with¦214974\add*.\x + \xo 33:4: \xt 2Ch 6:6.\x*
\v 5 He¦214990 built¦214980 altars¦214981 for all¦214982 the stars in the sky in both¦214986 of the temple courtyards¦214987,
\v 6 and he made¦214993 his¦214996 sons¦214996 pass¦214993 through the fire¦214997 in the Ben-Hinnom¦215001 valley¦214998, and he practiced¦215002 fortune-telling and sorcery¦215004 and magic, and \add ≈connected\add* with¦214997 mediums and spirits¦215007. He¦215008 did¦215009 so much evil¦215010 in Yahweh's¦215012 eyes¦215011 that it provoked¦215013 him¦215013 into getting angry.
\p
\v 7 He¦215015 made¦215021 an idol and put¦215042 it¦215015 in the¦215023 \add ≈temple\add*, \add yes,\add* where God¦215026 had¦215025 told David¦215029 and his son Shelomoh¦215032, “My name¦215045 \add ≈will be associated\add* with¦215034 that¦215020 building forever¦215046, there in Yerushalem¦215036, which¦215037 was chosen¦215038 from¦215039 among all¦215039 of¦215040 Yisrael's¦215041 tribes¦215040.\x + \xo 33:7-8: \xt 1Ki 9:3-5; 2Ch 7:12-18.\x*
\v 8 If Yisrael¦215054 will be careful to do¦215065 everything that I've instructed¦215070 them and follow all the regulations¦215074 and statutes that were given to them through Mosheh¦215078, then¦215048 I won't¦215048 force them to leave the land again that I gave to their ancestors¦215059.”
\v 9 \add ≈So¦215080\add* Menashsheh¦215081 led Yerushalem's¦215086 inhabitants¦215085 astray, and¦215085 \add all of\add* Yehudah¦215084—doing¦215087 more¦215089 evil¦215088 than¦215089 the nations¦215091 that¦215092 Yahweh¦215094 had¦215093 destroyed¦215093 ahead of the \add ≈Israelis¦215096\add*.
\rem /s1 Manasseh Restored after Repentance; Manasseh Repents
\p
\v 10 Then Yahweh¦215101 spoke¦215100 to¦215102 \add King\add* Menashsheh¦215104 and¦215105 his¦215107 people¦215107, \add ≈but¦215100\add* they¦215109 paid¦215109 no attention¦215109,
\v 11 \add ≈so\add* Yahweh¦215112 brought¦215111 in the Assyrian king's¦215119 army¦215117 leaders and they captured¦215121 Menashsheh¦215124. They put a hook \add in his nose\add* and bronze¦215127 chains around his feet, and they took¦215128 him¦215128 away to¦215129 Babylon¦215129.
\v 12 When¦215131 he was there suffering, he humbled¦215139 himself¦215139 in the¦215136 presence¦215141 of¦215136 his¦215138 ancestors' god¦215142 Yahweh¦215137, and¦215131 pleaded with him \add for help\add*.
\v 13 \add ≈When¦215145\add* he prayed¦215145, \add @Yahweh\add* listened¦215149 to him and answered his request and brought¦215151 him back to Yerushalem¦215152 to his kingdom¦215153. Then¦215145 Menashsheh¦215155 \add ≈realised\add* that¦215156 Yahweh \add really\add* is God.
\p
\v 14 After¦215161 that, he rebuilt¦215164 the outer¦215166 wall¦215165 for ‘The¦215168 City¦215168 of David¦215170’ on¦215173 the west of Gihon¦215172 to¦215174 the Fish¦215178 Gate¦215175 and¦215161 around¦215177 the hill—also making¦215179 it¦215179 higher. Then¦215161 he stationed army¦215184 commanders¦215182 in¦215173 all of Yehudah's¦215189 fortified¦215188 cities¦215187.
\v 15 He¦215191 removed¦215191 the foreign¦215195 gods¦215194 and idols from¦215199 the \add ≈temple\add*, as well as all the altars¦215203 that¦215204 he'd built¦215205 on¦215206 the \add ≈temple mount¦215206\add* and throughout Yerushalem¦215210, and he \add ≈dumped\add* them¦215211 \add all\add* outside¦215212 the city¦215213.
\v 16 Then¦215215 he rebuilt¦215215 Yahweh's¦215220 altar¦215219 and sacrificed¦215221 peace¦215224 offerings and thank¦215225 offerings¦215224 on it, and he told Yehudah¦215227 to serve¦215228 Yisrael's¦215233 god¦215232 Yahweh.
\v 17 However, the people¦215237 continued to offer sacrifices¦215238 at¦215239 the hilltop shrines¦215239, but¦215235 \add now\add* only¦215240 to their¦215242 god¦215242 Yahweh¦215241.
\rem /s1 The End of Manasseh's Reign; Death of Manasseh
\rem /r (2 Kings 21.17-18)
\p
\v 18 The record of all the other things Menashsheh¦215246 did \add ≈while he was king\add*, including¦215247 his prayer¦215247 to his god¦215250 and¦215244 the messages¦215251 spoken¦215253 to him¦215254 by the prophets, was written on¦215260 the scroll ‘The kings¦215263 of Yisrael¦215258’.
\v 19 It's¦215269 written¦215285 in ‘The Words of Hozay’ about¦215266 his disobedience and unfaithful¦215273 activities, and how he built¦215276 the hilltop¦215278 shrines¦215274 and Asherah¦215280 \add poles\add* and idols¦215281 before he humbled¦215283 himself¦215283, as well as \add ≈the response to his pleading in his prayer¦215266\add*.
\v 20 Then¦215290 Menashsheh¦215291 \add ≈died\add* and was buried¦215295 at his \add ≈palace\add*, and his son¦215299 Amon¦215298 \add ≈replaced him¦215295 as king¦215297\add*.
\s1 Yehudah's King Amon
\rem /s1 Amon's Reign and Death; King Amon of Judah; Amon Rules in Judah; Amon King of Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 21:19-26)
\p
\v 21 Amon¦215308 was¦215303 twenty-two¦215305 when¦215309 he became¦215309 king¦215309, and¦215310 he ruled from Yerushalem¦215313 for two years¦215311.
\v 22 He did what Yahweh¦215318 \add ≈had said was\add* evil¦215316, just like his father Menashsheh¦215321 had done¦215320, and \add *he¦215327\add* sacrificed¦215330 to¦215323 all¦215323 the¦215325 idols¦215325 his father had made, and served¦215332 them¦215332.
\v 23 He¦215339 didn't¦215334 humble himself¦215335 \add ≈and¦215334 turn to¦215336 Yahweh¦215337 like his¦215340 father¦215340 had¦215338\add*, \add ≈so Amon's¦215343 guilt¦215345 was even worse\add*.
\p
\v 24 Then¦215347 \add @Amon's\add* servants¦215349 conspired¦215347 against him, and¦215347 assassinated him in¦215351 his¦215351 \add ≈palace\add*,
\v 25 \add ≈but¦215353 the local\add* people executed everyone¦215354 who'd conspired¦215360 against King¦215363 Amon¦215364, and¦215353 made¦215365 his son Yoshiyah¦215371 king in his place¦215373.
\c 34
\s1 Yehudah's King Yoshiyah
\rem /s1 Reign of Josiah; Josiah's reforms; Josiah's Reforms; King Josiah of Judah; Josiah Rules in Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Kgs 22:1-2)
\p
\v 1 Yoshiyah¦215380 was¦215376 eight¦215378 when¦215381 he became¦215381 king¦215381, and he reigned¦215385 from Yerushalem¦215386 for thirty-one¦215382 years¦215379.\x + \xo 34:1: \xt Jer 3:6.\x*
\v 2 He¦215388 did what Yahweh¦215391 \add ≈had said was\add* right, and¦215388 \add ≈made¦215388 similar decisions to his ancestor David¦215394\add*—not deviating to the right or the left¦215400.
\rem /s1 Josiah Attacks Pagan Worship
\p
\v 3 In¦215402 the eighth¦215402 year of his reign¦215404, while he was¦215407 still¦215406 a \add ≈teenager\add*, he began to \add ≈learn more\add* about¦215404 the god¦215410 of his ancestor David¦215411, and in¦215402 the twelfth year, he began to remove the hilltop¦215424 shrines¦215424 from Yerushalem¦215421 and \add across\add* Yehudah¦215420, as well as the Asherah¦215425 poles¦215425, and the \add ≈carved and cast metal idols¦215426\add*.
\v 4 \add ≈He watched while\add* they tore down the altars for the Baals¦215433 and cut¦215439 down the incense¦215434 altars that¦215435 were¦215437 above¦215437 them. He shattered the Asherah¦215440 poles¦215440 and the \add ≈idols¦215441\add*, then¦215429 crushed them \add to powder\add*, which¦215435 he¦215445 scattered¦215445 over the graves¦215449 of¦215448 \add ≈the people¦215429\add* who'd sacrificed¦215450 to them.\x + \xo 34:4: \xt 2Ki 21:3; 2Ch 33:3.\x*
\v 5 He burnt¦215455 the¦215453 bones¦215453 of \add those\add* priests¦215454 on¦215456 their altars, and \add that's how\add* he¦215455 purified Yerushalem¦215466 and \add all of\add* Yehudah¦215463,\x + \xo 34:5: \xt 1Ki 13:2.\x*
\v 6 as well as the cities¦215468 in \add the tribes of\add* Menashsheh¦215469, Efrayim¦215470, Shimeon¦215471, and¦215468 \add as far north as\add* Naftali¦215474, including¦215470 in the ruins¦215476 around¦215478 them.
\v 7 He¦215480 tore down the altars and the Asherah¦215486 poles¦215486, ground the idols¦215487 into pieces, and cut¦215493 down the incense¦215492 altars in¦215494 all¦215494 of¦215496 Yisrael¦215497, \add ≈before returning¦215498\add* to¦215499 Yerushalem¦215499.
\rem /s1 The Book of the Law Is Discovered; Discovery of the Book of the Law
\rem /r (2 Kings 22.3-20)
\p
\v 8 In¦215502 his eighteenth¦215503 year¦215502 \add ≈as king\add*, in order¦215506 to purify the land¦215507 and the \add ≈temple\add*, he¦215509 sent¦215509 Atsalyah's son Shafan¦215512, Maaseyah¦215518 the city¦215521 official¦215519, and Yoahaz's son Yoah¦215523 the secretary, to repair¦215528 his god¦215533 Yahweh's¦215532 residence.
\v 9 They¦215535 went¦215535 to¦215536 Hilkiyah¦215538 the high¦215541 priest¦215540 and \add ≈handed over the funds that¦215550 had been donated\add* for God's¦215549 house¦215547, which¦215550 had been collected by the Levites¦215553 as doorkeepers—\add the money\add* was from \add the tribes of\add* Menashsheh¦215557, Efrayim¦215558, and other places in \add the northern kingdom of\add* Yisrael¦215561, as well as from Yerushalem's¦215568 inhabitants and \add people across\add* all¦215562 of Yehudah¦215564 and Benyamin¦215565.
\v 10 \add @The¦215586 priests\add* then¦215570 \add ≈paid the workers\add* who'd been¦215576 assigned to \add work in\add* Yahweh's¦215578 \add ≈temple\add* to repair¦215587 and¦215570 strengthen it.
\v 11 They¦215591 also \add ≈paid the carpenters¦215592\add* and builders¦215593 to buy cut stone¦215595 and timber¦215597 for¦215598 the beams¦215599 and braces which¦215603 Yehudah's¦215606 kings¦215605 \add ≈had¦215604 allowed to deteriorate\add*,
\v 12 and the \add ≈workers\add* did their work¦215611 faithfully¦215610. \add ≈Their supervisors¦215628\add* were Yahat¦215615 and Ovadyah¦215616 (Levites descended¦215620 from Merari¦215621), as well as Zekaryah¦215622 and Meshullam¦215623 (descendants¦215626 of \add Levi's son\add* Kohat), and all the Levites who¦215632 \add ≈were good on¦215610 their\add* musical instruments¦215633.
\v 13 They \add also\add* supervised¦215639 the porters¦215638 and \add ≈people\add* working¦215642 on various \add other\add* tasks. Some¦215645 of¦215645 the Levites¦215645 were¦215646 secretaries, officials¦215647, and guards.
\p
\rem /s1 The Book of the Law Found; Hilkiah Discovers God's Law
\v 14 When¦215650 they¦215650 were bringing¦215650 the money¦215653 out of the \add ≈temple\add*, Hilkiyah¦215658 the \add high\add* priest¦215659 found¦215657 a scroll¦215662 containing Yahweh's¦215656 instructions that¦215654 had¦215654 \add ≈been given via\add* Mosheh¦215668 \add !(Moses¦215668)\add*.
\v 15 Hilkiyah¦215671 \add ≈informed\add* the scribe¦215676 Shafan¦215675 that he'd found¦215679 a Torah scroll in the \add ≈temple\add*, and¦215670 \add *he\add* gave¦215682 \add *him\add* the scroll.
\v 16 Shafan¦215692 took the scroll¦215695 \add with him\add* to the king and¦215691 \add ≈reported¦215699\add* to him, “Your¦215712 servants¦215712 are¦215714 doing¦215714 everything they¦215713 were assigned to do.
\v 17 They've taken the cash¦215719 that¦215720 was¦215720 in¦215721 Yahweh's¦215723 residence, and¦215716 \add ≈passed it¦215724 on\add* to the supervisors \add ≈of the workers\add*.”
\v 18 Then¦215735 Shafan¦215736 told¦215735 the king, “\add I've brought\add* a scroll¦215740 that the priest¦215744 Hilkiyah¦215743 gave¦215741 me,” and¦215735 then¦215735 \add *he¦215745\add* started reading it out to¦215749 the king.
\p
\v 19 \add ≈When¦215752\add* the king¦215754 heard¦215753 the words¦215756 \add ≈of¦215756 God's instructions\add*, he¦215758 tore¦215758 his¦215761 clothes¦215761 \add in grief\add*.
\v 20 Then¦215763 \add *he¦215763\add* commanded¦215763 Hilkiyah¦215767, Shafan's¦215773 son Ahikam¦215770, Mikah's¦215779 son Avdon¦215776, Shafan \add himself\add*, and¦215763 the king's¦215788 special advisor Asayah¦215785,
\v 21 “Go¦215791 \add and\add* \add ≈ask\add* Yahweh on my behalf, and on behalf of \add ≈everyone¦215827\add* in¦215799 Yisrael¦215799 and Yehudah¦215800, \add ≈what he wants us to do¦215826\add* with¦215799 respect to the instructions on the scroll that's \add just\add* been¦215806 found¦215806, because \add it seems that\add* Yahweh \add ≈will be very angry at¦215799 us\add* because our¦215821 ancestors¦215821 haven't \add ≈followed all of Yahweh's¦215795 instructions as they're\add* written¦215829 on that scroll.”
\rem /s1 The Prophet Huldah Consulted
\p
\v 22 \add ≈So\add* Hilkiyah¦215837 and \add ≈the others\add* went¦215836 to Yerushalem's¦215858 Second District, to the prophetess¦215843 Huldah¦215842 (wife¦215844 of Shallum¦215846, son of Tokahat, son of Hasrah¦215853 who \add ≈took care of the temple robes\add*). They¦215860 \add ≈passed on¦215858 the king's question\add*,
\v 23 and¦215865 she¦215865 \add ≈replied¦215865\add*, “Yisrael's¦215872 god¦215871 Yahweh¦215870 says¦215869 to¦215866 tell \add ≈the¦215871 king\add*:
\v 24 Yahweh¦215884 says¦215883, ‘Listen, I'm¦215885 going to bring¦215886 disaster on this¦215891 place¦215890 and¦215892 its¦215894 inhabitants¦215894, as per all¦215896 the¦215898 curses¦215898 written¦215899 on that scroll¦215902 that was read¦215904 to Yehudah's¦215907 king¦215906,
\v 25 because they've¦215912 abandoned¦215912 me and offered incense¦215913 to¦215916 other¦215917 gods¦215916. \add That's\add* made¦215913 me angry, and \add so\add* my rage¦215924 will be poured¦215923 out on¦215920 this¦215926 place¦215925, and it¦215928 \add ≈can't¦215927 be prevented\add*.’
\v 26 \add ≈However,\add* Yehudah's¦215933 king¦215932 sent¦215934 you¦215950 all to \add ≈ask for Yahweh's¦215937 direction\add*, so¦215938 tell him¦215940, ‘Yisrael's¦215947 god¦215946 Yahweh says¦215944 that¦215949 \add after\add* \add ≈hearing¦215950\add* \add the scroll's\add* words¦215948,
\v 27 \add ≈you were sincere\add*, and you humbled yourself in front of God¦215959 when¦215960 you heard his¦215963 words¦215963 about¦215955 this¦215967 place¦215966 and its¦215970 inhabitants¦215970. \add Yes,\add* Yahweh¦215985 declares you've humbled yourself in front of me and torn¦215973 your¦215976 clothes¦215976, and wept¦215977 \add ≈in¦215960 front of\add* me, and I've¦215982 \add ≈personally\add* heard you.
\v 28 \add ≈So listen¦215987,\add* I'll \add ≈allow you¦215988 to die and be¦215992 buried in¦215996 peace¦215996\add*, and \add so\add* your¦215988 eyes¦216000 won't¦215997 see the¦216016 disaster¦216002 that I'll¦216004 \add ≈send\add* to this¦216009 place¦216008 and its¦216012 inhabitants¦216012.’ ”
\m \add ≈So\add* they¦215999 \add ≈took that response\add* back¦216013 to¦215989 the king¦216016.
\rem /s1 Josiah Makes a Covenant to Obey the Lord; The Covenant Renewed; Josiah's Religious Reforms
